{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And that they proclaim</b> — \"olives of oil-wood\" [עלי עץ שמן], \"leaves of myrtle-wood\" [עלי עץ הדס], and \"leaves of palms\" [עלי תמרים] — the vavs add nothing to the meaning; only their accent [te'amim]. Either \"leaves of oil-wood\" or \"leaves of myrtle.\" Likewise \"and one who curses his father and his mother.\" The myrtle [hadas] and the thick-leafed tree [avot] we know from tradition to be one tree, and likewise the translator [Targum] rendered: \"thick myrtles\" [עבות הדסין], in abbreviated form. Only there are species in which three leaves are found on one stem; this is what is called of the thick-leafed type [min ma'aseh avot]. I have found one with six leaves, and it is the finest of all to be called avot. Some say it is found with seven leaves. Our Sages called it: the foolish myrtle."], :book :nehemiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "The opening phrase 'ואשר ישמיעו' appears to be Ibn Ezra's lemma heading introducing the verse citation that follows; rendered as a bold lemma per convention."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Nehemiah -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/nechemya"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:15:27.757296728Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}